<ClinicNote slug="achilles-tendinopathy">
  <p>Your Achilles is the thick tendon joining the calf to the heel. Tendinopathy means it has become painful and less able to cope with load. It comes in two main types:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Mid-portion:</b> pain about 2 to 6 cm above the heel. Most common in runners and active people.</li>
    <li><b>Insertional:</b> pain right where the tendon attaches to the heel bone. It can happen at any age or activity level.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The big myth: complete rest</h2>
  <p>Tendons get stronger when they're loaded, and they lose capacity when they're not. The 2024 clinical practice guideline for mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy is clear:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Clinicians should use tendon loading exercise, with loads as high as tolerated, as a first-line treatment."</p><cite>Chimenti et al., JOSPT Clinical Practice Guideline, 2024 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>The same guideline advises that complete rest is not indicated. A randomised trial found people who kept running and training while monitoring their pain did just as well as those who rested:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"No negative effects could be demonstrated from continuing Achilles tendon-loading activity."</p><cite>Silbernagel et al., Am J Sports Med, 2007 [3]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>Which exercises?</h2>
  <p>Slow, heavy calf raises done about three times a week worked as well as the classic daily heel-drop programme, and people stuck with them better (92% vs 78% of sessions completed) [2]. For insertional pain, we usually keep the heel above floor level early on, so the tendon isn't squashed against the bone.</p>
  <h2>What about injections and gadgets?</h2>
  <p>In a double-blind trial, a platelet-rich plasma (PRP) injection was no better than a saltwater injection when both groups also did exercise [4]. A 2026 meta-analysis found shockwave "showed no clear overall benefits compared to sham" [6]. Researchers now recommend giving exercise alone a proper trial of around three months before adding extras [5].</p>

  <Fragment slot="side">
    <div class="cn-tldr"><h2>In 30 seconds</h2><ul>
      <li>Morning stiffness that eases as you move is the classic sign.</li>
      <li>Progressive calf loading is first-line care. Complete rest isn't.</li>
      <li>Expect around 12 weeks of consistent work. Tendons are slow, and slow is fast.</li>
    </ul></div>
